faqs:
  - q: "My house already has hardwired alarms. Do they expire too?"
    a: "They do, and this catches a lot of people out. Being wired to the mains keeps the power supply sorted, but the smoke sensor inside still degrades — 10 years is the limit, same as battery alarms. Loads of 2000s-era Christchurch homes, including plenty of post-quake rebuilds from the early 2010s, are running hardwired alarms that are now past their date. Check the sticker on the side; if there isn't one, it's almost certainly overdue."
  - q: "Can I replace a hardwired alarm myself?"
    a: "No — and not just as a legal technicality. Hardwired alarms connect to 240V mains wiring, which is restricted electrical work that needs a registered electrician. DIY mains work risks a shock, a fire (grimly ironic in this case), your insurance cover, and problems at sale time. The good news: a straight swap is quick for us and priced accordingly."
  - q: "Hardwired or long-life battery — which is actually better?"
    a: "Honest answer: for most existing Christchurch homes, modern sealed-battery photoelectric alarms are just as good and cheaper to install — that's what we recommend when there's no wiring in place already. Where hardwired wins: homes that already have the wiring (replacements are easy), new builds where the Code requires it, and larger interconnected systems. If your house has hardwired alarms now, stick with hardwired — replacing like for like keeps everything compliant."
  - q: "Do I get a Certificate of Compliance?"
    a: "Yes. Any wired alarm work we do comes with a Certificate of Compliance issued by the electrician who did the job, confirming the work meets the wiring rules. Keep it with your house records — insurers and lawyers ask for these at claim and sale time."
